  "plan": {
    "title": "Week 1: fast runs, rich chords",
    "summary": "Your runs go from slow and clean to sixteenths, one stage at a time: five notes, turns, the octave, pentatonic runs, then runs aimed at the chords. On guitar, 9th chords and shells on a ii–V–I in G and C give your voice a richer bed, and most days end with you singing over a backing track.",
    "progress": "Runs: started from clean slow runs. This week: five-note, nine-note, octave and pentatonic runs (hard days 1-3, expert from day 4), sixteenths on the click from 54 up to 80, turns on the 3rd, falling minor pentatonic run, runs aimed at the 3rd of the next chord. Check ratings for where sixteenths blur. Next: longer runs across the break, triplet runs, runs in a real song.\nExtended chords: knew 7th, sus, add9. This week: Am9, D9, Gmaj7, Dm9, G9, Cmaj7, shells on the A-D-G strings, turnaround in C and G, sixteenth strumming with misses up to 112, comping over drums and bass. Next: 13ths, shells with the root on the low E, chord-melody fills between vocal lines.\nCAGED: plays one chord in five shapes. This week: Am, D, G triads on the top two string sets only. Next: the other two sets, inversions along one set.\nIntervals & leaps: had 4ths, 5ths, octaves. This week: 7ths, tritone, minor 6th from a neighbour note, one session; tunes with 7ths, octave, tenths. Next: the leaps down, no dot.\nHarmony & improvising: sang a harmony line already. This week: guide tones over ii–V–I in G, C, F, two harmony lines over I-V-vi-IV, Dorian vamp aiming for the 6th. Next: harmony a 3rd above a melody, minor ii–V.\nBreath & range: strong; short warm-ups only (farinelli to 10, lip rolls, hills). Keep as warm-up.\nPitch, Melodies: strong, not drilled alone.\nScales, Barre chords, Basic chords, Techniques: strong, only used inside solos and comping. Next week a little Dorian soloing with bends.\nEar (not answered): tested gently with 7th chords by ear and intervals in two stages; read the ratings before building on it.",
